WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:12.560
Today, we are able to take a bunch of photos,&nbsp;
and use an AI to magically, create a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.120
where we can fly through these photos. It is&nbsp;
really crazy, because this is possible today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.760
for instance, here is NVIDIAs method that can be&nbsp;
trained to perform this in a matter of seconds.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:34.000
Now, I said that in these we can fly through&nbsp;
these photos. But here is an insane idea:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:42.080
what if we used not multiple photos, but just&nbsp;
one photo, and we don’t fly through it, but fly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.360
into this photo. Now, you are probably asking,&nbsp;
Károly, what are you talking about? This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:55.440
is completely insane, and it wouldn’t work with&nbsp;
these NERF-based solutions like the one you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:02.320
here, these were not designed to&nbsp;
do this at all! Look. Oh yes. That.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:10.480
So, in order to fly into these photos, we&nbsp;
would to invent at least 3 things. One,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.360
image inpainting. Look, if we are to fly into&nbsp;
this photo, we will have to be able to look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:24.800
regions between the trees. Unfortunately, these&nbsp;
are not part of the original photo, and hence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:31.440
new content needs to be generated intelligently.&nbsp;
That is a formidable task for an AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:37.200
and luckily, image inpainting techniques&nbsp;
already exist out there. Here’s one.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.480
But inpainting is not nearly&nbsp;
enough. Two. As we fly into a photo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:50.000
completely new regions should also&nbsp;
appear that are beyond the image. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.440
means that we also need to perform image&nbsp;
outpainting, creating these new regions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.040
Continuing the image, if you will. Luckily, we are&nbsp;
entering the age of AI-driven image generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.840
and this is also possible today, for&nbsp;
instance, with this incredible tool.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:16.720
But even that is not enough. Why is that? Well,&nbsp;
three! As we fly closer to these new regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.480
we will be looking at fewer and fewer pixels&nbsp;
and from closer and closer, which means…this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:32.080
Oh my, another problem. So, we surely can’t solve&nbsp;
this, right? Well, great news - we can! Here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.680
Google’s diffusion-based solution to super&nbsp;
resolution, where the principle is simple:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:44.480
have a look at this technique from last year. In&nbsp;
goes a course image or video, and this AI-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:51.920
method is tasked with…this! Yes. This is not&nbsp;
science fiction. This is super resolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.840
where the AI starts out from noise and&nbsp;
synthesizes crisp details onto the image.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:06.240
So, this might not be such an insane idea after&nbsp;
all! So, does the fact we can do all three of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:13.680
these separately mean that this task is easy?&nbsp;
Well, let’s see how previous techniques were able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:20.320
to tackle this challenge. My guess is that&nbsp;
this is still sinfully difficult to do. And…oh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:28.240
boy. Well, I see a lot of glitches and not a lot&nbsp;
of new, meaningful content being synthesized here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.800
And note these are not some ancient techniques,&nbsp;
these are all from just two years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:42.640
It really seems there is not a lot of hope here.&nbsp;
And now, hold on to your papers, and let’s see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:48.720
how Google’s new AI puts all of these&nbsp;
together, and lets us fly into this photo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:57.760
Wow, this is so much better! I love it.
Clearly, not perfect, but I feel that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:02.960
is the first work where the flying into&nbsp;
photos concept really comes into life.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:10.800
And it has a bunch of really cool features too,&nbsp;
for instance, one, it can generate even longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.280
videos, which means that after a few seconds,&nbsp;
everything that we see is synthesized by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:25.680
AI. Two, it supports not only this boring linear&nbsp;
camera motion, but these really cool, curvy camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.600
trajectories too. Putting these two features&nbsp;
together, we can get these cool animations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:37.600
that were not possible before this paper. Now,&nbsp;
the flaws are clearly visible for everyone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:43.600
but this is a historic episode where we can&nbsp;
invoke the three Laws of Papers to address them.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:49.360
The First Law Of Papers says that research&nbsp;
is a process. Do not look at where we are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.080
look at where we will be two more&nbsp;
papers down the line. With this concept,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.080 --> 00:05:01.600
we are roughly where DALL-E 1 was about a year&nbsp;
ago. That is an image generator AI that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:10.720
produce images of this quality. And, just one year&nbsp;
later, DALL-E 2 arrived, which could do this! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:17.360
just imagine what kind of videos this will be&nbsp;
able to create just one more paper down the line.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.600
The Second Law of Papers says&nbsp;
that everything is connected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:27.120
This AI technique is able to learn&nbsp;
image inpainting, image outpainting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.840
and super resolution techniques at the same time,&nbsp;
and even combine them creatively. We don’t need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:40.320
3 separate AIs to do these, just one&nbsp;
technique. That is very impressive.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:47.200
And finally, the Third Law Of Papers says&nbsp;
that a bad researcher fails 100% of the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:54.960
while a good one only fails 99% of the time.&nbsp;
Hence, what you see here is always just 1%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:02.080
of the work that was done. Why is that? Well, for&nbsp;
instance, this is a neural network-based solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:08.960
which means that we need a ton of training data&nbsp;
for these AIs to learn on. And hence, scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:15.200
at Google also needed to create a technique to&nbsp;
gather a ton of drone videos on the internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:22.720
and create a clean dataset also with labelings as&nbsp;
well. The labels are essentially depth information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:29.200
which shows how far different parts of the image&nbsp;
are from the camera. And they did it for more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.200 --> 00:06:36.880
than 2 million images in total. So, once again,&nbsp;
if you include all the versions of this idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.840
that didn’t work, what you see here&nbsp;
is just 1% of the work that was done.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:49.760
And now, we can not only fly through photos, but&nbsp;
also fly into photos. What a time to be alive!

